  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}-15$, where $t$ is measured in seconds.  Find the particle's acceleration at $t=3$ seconds.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}+20t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}+20t) \\\\  & =24t+20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=3$, we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(3) & =24(3)+20 \\\\  & =72+20 \\\\  & =92\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=3$ seconds is $92\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$",
